{"entries":{"chapter-95-study-hematologic-malignancies-and-plasma-cell-disorders|Chapter 95 Study: Hematologic Malignancies and Plasma Cell Disorders":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"definition-amp-diagnosis|Definition & diagnosis":["u3"],"copy|MDS: clonal ineffective hematopoiesis with cytopenias; it may remain indolent or progress to marrow failure/AML. Older patients' outcomes reflect both disease biology and fitness. (p. 1483)":["u4"],"copy|Suspect MDS with progressive macrocytic anemia/cytopenias; exclude mimics and confirm with marrow biopsy plus cytogenetics. (p. 1484)":["u5"],"copy|CLL: peripheral-blood flow cytometry establishes the characteristic clonal population; marrow studies are usually unnecessary for diagnosis. (p. 1490)":["u6"],"copy|Lymphoma requires tissue for classification; fine-needle aspiration is inadequate for subtype determination. (p. 1494)":["u7"],"copy|Non-IgM MGUS: monoclonal protein <30 g/L, marrow plasma cells <10%, without related end-organ damage; assess calcium, renal function, anemia and bone lesions. (p. 1503)":["u8"],"management|Management":["u9"],"copy|MDS treatment is risk-adapted; supportive care is central for lower-risk/frail patients. Erythropoietin response is likelier with lower-risk disease, low transfusion burden and serum erythropoietin <500 mU/mL. (p. 1485)":["u10"],"copy|CLL leukocytosis alone does not require therapy; symptoms/progressive cytopenias, molecular risk and fitness guide treatment. (p. 1491)":["u11"],"copy|MGUS needs follow-up rather than treatment; smoldering myeloma requires closer monitoring, with emerging lenalidomide benefit in intermediate/high-risk disease. (p. 1503)":["u12"],"drugs-amp-cautions|Drugs & cautions":["u13"],"copy|Azacitidine/decitabine: myelosuppression may worsen for months before response; azacitidine has survival evidence, whereas decitabine lacks definitive randomized survival benefit. (p. 1485)":["u14"],"copy|BTK inhibitors for CLL: bleeding/anticoagulant caution and cardiovascular toxicity, including atrial fibrillation. (p. 1491)":["u15"],"copy|Myeloma trial: dexamethasone 40 mg orally weekly with lenalidomide had better overall survival than the high-dose schedule despite lower response; high-dose toxicity included thrombosis and infections. (p. 1504)":["u16"],"copy|Lenalidomide needs renal dose adjustment; bortezomib does not. Weekly subcutaneous bortezomib is better tolerated than the older twice-weekly intravenous schedule; neuropathy and zoster matter. (p. 1505)":["u17"],"exam-traps|Exam traps":["u18"],"copy|Anemia or renal dysfunction from another established cause does not itself fulfill myeloma-related organ damage. (p. 1503)":["u19"],"copy|Bone scans are generally negative in myeloma because lesions are purely lytic. (p. 1504)":["u20"],"recall|Recall":["u21"],"past-paper-questions-on-this-chapter|Past-paper questions on this chapter":["u22"],"copy|2023-Jun Q18 — mcq-bd9893fe8c4027b4668a863e":["u23"],"copy|2024-May Q12 — mcq-834756aabc08598d8f45a7f5":["u24"],"copy|2024-May Q85 — mcq-d7ef5d7a467c8ade6436f4f5":["u25"],"copy|2026-Jun Q99 — mcq-c0958b48b6b8fb43106b759b":["u26"],"table-95-1-book-image|Table 95-1 — book image":["u27"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, Table 95-1, p.1485":["u28","u48"],"copy|CHAPTER 95s / HAZZARD":["u29"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 95; the book is the reference.":["u30"],"copy|Full chapter available: Read Chapter 95.":["u31"],"copy|Fast build from the book; not lane-reviewed.":["u32"],"copy|3.":["u33"],"copy|Which patients with MDS are more likely to benefit from erythropoietin?":["u34"],"copy|5.":["u35"],"copy|What is the lenalidomide-responsive cytogenetic MDS subset, and what cautions matter?":["u36"],"copy|7.":["u37"],"copy|What is critical for successful oral TKI treatment of CML?":["u38"],"copy|10.":["u39"],"copy|What BTK-inhibitor risks matter in an anticoagulated patient with CLL?":["u40"],"copy|11.":["u41"],"copy|How should new anemia in CLL be evaluated for hemolysis?":["u42"],"copy|13.":["u43"],"copy|What are the protein and marrow thresholds for non-IgM MGUS?":["u44"],"copy|15.":["u45"],"copy|How do renal dosing requirements differ for lenalidomide and bortezomib?":["u46"],"copy|2024-May Q12":["u47"],"copy|Answer":["u49","u53","u57","u59","u61","u63","u69"],"copy|Those with lower-risk scores, serum erythropoietin":["u50"],"copy|<500 mU/mL":["u51"],"copy|, low transfusion requirements and a shorter interval from diagnosis to treatment. (p. 1485)":["u52"],"copy|Transfusion-dependent disease with deletion of the long arm of chromosome":["u54"],"copy|5":["u55"],"copy|as the sole abnormality; watch myelosuppression and adjust dosing for renal impairment. (p. 1486)":["u56"],"copy|Daily adherence and careful response monitoring; PCR provides sensitive monitoring, and comorbid cardiac/thrombotic risks inform drug selection. (p. 1490)":["u58"],"copy|Increased bleeding risk, plus cardiovascular toxicities such as atrial fibrillation, heart failure and hypertension. (p. 1491)":["u60"],"copy|Reticulocyte count and direct Coombs testing help distinguish hemolysis from decreased marrow production. (p. 1492)":["u62"],"copy|Serum monoclonal protein":["u64"],"copy|<30 g/L":["u65"],"copy|and marrow plasma cells":["u66"],"copy|<10%":["u67"],"copy|, without related end-organ damage or amyloidosis. (p. 1503)":["u68"],"copy|Lenalidomide requires dose adjustment for renal insufficiency; bortezomib does not. (p. 1505)":["u70"],"copy|Exam topics link to the marked chapter and the corresponding questions.":["u32"],"copy|MGUS is monitored, and smoldering myeloma warrants closer surveillance and assessment of whether its progression risk supports early treatment. (p. 1503)":["u12"],"copy|Table 95-14 prints 10%–60% clonal marrow plasma cells for smoldering myeloma with no attributable end-organ damage, and >60% for myeloma when CRAB criteria are absent.":["u231"],"copy|Hazzard describes close surveillance of smoldering disease and early-treatment trials (pp1502–1504).":["u134"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): IMWG uses ≥60% clonal marrow plasma cells as a myeloma-defining event.":["u131"],"copy|Additional SLiM biomarkers are the involved/uninvolved free light-chain ratio ≥100 with involved light chain ≥100 mg/L, or >1 MRI focal lesion of ≥5 mm.":["u133"],"copy|In 2025 FDA approved subcutaneous daratumumab with hyaluronidase for adults with high-risk smoldering myeloma.":["u135"],"copy|Hazzard lists zoledronic acid and pamidronate and describes denosumab use when renal impairment contraindicates a bisphosphonate.":["u232"],"copy|Calcium and vitamin D alone cannot prevent skeletal complications (p1505).":["u144"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): ASCO also supports denosumab to prevent skeletal events in myeloma and notes its renal safety advantage in selected patients.":["u142"],"copy|The oncology regimen differs from the Prolia regimen named in the distractor.":["u233"],"copy|The exam credits the Burkitt pairing, also shown in Table 95-9 (p1494).":["u172"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): The ICC classifies BCR::ABL1-positive B-cell ALL.":["u234"],"copy|Some myelomas acquire secondary MYC rearrangements, which explains the ambiguity in a question asking about any association.":["u235"],"copy|Fine-needle aspiration may suggest lymphoma but is not adequate for identifying lymphoma subtype (p1494).":["u179"]},"maxId":235,"sequenceOffsets":[[236,-165],[296,-159],[301,-156],[327,-153],[332,-152],[383,0]]}
